<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Do olive oil drops really work for earwax?</h3>
<p>Olive oil can soften and lubricate wax, which may reduce irritation and make professional removal easier. It doesn’t always clear a blockage on its own. If your hearing stays muffled after several days of softening, it’s time for an audiology or ENT visit.</p>

<h3>Is hydrogen peroxide safe to use in my ears?</h3>
<p>Low-strength peroxide can help soften wax for some people, but avoid it if you have a suspected eardrum perforation, ear tubes, or signs of infection. It may temporarily increase fullness as it bubbles. If you’re uncertain about your eardrum status, skip home treatments and see a professional.</p>

<h3>Can earwax cause tinnitus or dizziness?</h3>
<p>Yes—impacted wax can trigger temporary ringing, fullness, and even brief dizziness, especially when water gets trapped behind it. These symptoms often improve once the blockage is safely removed. If they persist, seek an audiology evaluation.</p>

<h3>Are ear candles safe?</h3>
<p>No. Studies show ear candles do not remove wax and can cause burns, ear canal injury, and eardrum perforations. Regulatory agencies advise against their use. Choose evidence-based options instead.</p>


